From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 23:52:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BD-J titles play in the isolated BD-J host, never in the
 player's process

With the remote code built (Linux), a disc whose index has BD-J titles is handed over to the host
when the player starts it with its menus: bd_play(), bd_play_title() of the first play, and
bd_start_bdj(). From then on every public function that reads or changes the playback state goes
there (bd_read(), bd_read_ext(), the events, seeks and positions, titles, angles, streams, user
input, rate, menu call, player settings, overlay registrations, bd_get_clpi() through the clip
name). What only reads the disc stays in the player, which parses its own copy as before: disc
info, metadata, title and playlist info, sound effects, files. A disc without BD-J titles, or one
played by playlist, does not change at all.

The player settings given before the hand-over are recorded and replayed in the host in the same
order, those given before the disc was opened before the host opens it; the host keeps its own
Java, cache and persistent storage. BLURAY_DISC_INFO says it (bdj_remote), and libjvm_detected and
bdj_handled then say whether the host can be reached (its socket, /run/hades-bdj/bdj.sock), not
whether a JVM loads here.

No Java VM starts in the player's process: if the host is not there or can not start the disc,
its BD-J titles do not run (BD_ERROR_BDJ), and the rest of the disc plays as before.
BLURAY_PLAYER_SETTING_BDJ_REMOTE (BLURAY_BDJ_REMOTE_NEVER) is for the host itself, before the disc
is opened.
